What is proactive risk management in power plants?
Proactive risk management means identifying hazards, assessing changing conditions and verifying controls before an incident occurs. It combines planned assessments with ongoing learning from inspections, near misses, maintenance activities and worker observations.

AI can support this process by analysing information across records and highlighting patterns that deserve attention. It does not replace competent people, physical safeguards or established work procedures.

The goal is practical: give the right person useful information early enough to make a difference.

Reactive reporting and proactive management work together
Incident reporting remains essential. A thorough investigation can explain what happened, identify contributing factors and guide corrective action.

The opportunity is to apply that learning more widely.

If an investigation identifies a recurring access problem around one turbine, could similar conditions exist around another? If a control failed during a maintenance activity, should comparable work be reviewed before it begins?

A proactive approach carries those questions into daily planning.

Area	Reactive reporting focus	Proactive risk management focus
Incident information	Understanding an event after it occurs	Applying the learning to similar activities
Inspection findings	Recording individual deficiencies	Reviewing recurrence and unresolved concerns
Corrective actions	Tracking completion	Verifying effectiveness and checking for recurrence
Work planning	Reviewing the record of a completed task	Considering relevant findings before authorisation
Management review	Looking at historical outcomes	Examining current conditions alongside past performance
The strongest approach connects both perspectives. It learns from events while continually checking whether existing controls remain effective.

Where AI can add practical value
1. Connect reports that describe the same concern differently
One employee reports “poor access.” Another writes “materials blocking the route.” A third records “difficulty reaching the inspection point.”

A keyword search may treat these as separate issues. AI-assisted text analysis can help identify a shared theme and group the records for review.

The reviewer then checks the location, equipment, timing and circumstances. Similar wording does not automatically mean the same cause—but it can provide a useful starting point.

