4. Identify recurrence after an action is closed
An action marked “completed” is not always evidence that the underlying concern has been resolved.

Suppose a recurring housekeeping issue is closed after materials are removed. If similar reports return every week, the team may need to examine storage arrangements, delivery timing or work-area planning.

AI-assisted comparison can help bring those repeated observations together. People must then investigate the reasons and determine whether the control needs to change.

This is an important shift: from asking whether an action was completed to asking whether it worked.

5. Make safety information easier to review
Plant leaders need a clear view of what requires attention, supported by the underlying evidence.

A useful AI-assisted summary might identify recurring findings in a particular area, highlight unresolved actions and link back to the original records. It should distinguish recorded facts from suggested interpretations.

